EMIT

A case-branch is a third OPENER beside a dyn-with-body and a nested application argument, and it
brackets itself for the reason the rule exists: the statement after the last branch belongs to the
enclosing block, and whitespace cannot say so. The header is `case`, the binder if bound, the type
if typed, `[`; then statements, the `=` result, `]`. An absent binder or type leaves NO marker —
case alone tells the four forms apart, the rule that already bounds a reference.

PARSE

`case` joins the statement keywords a dyn body admits, and `message` — alone of the three actor
blocks, since start and signal receive nothing to dispatch on — admits it too. The arm reads up to
two tokens before `[`: an upper-case initial is the type, lower-case the binder, in either order.
Then statements, result, `]`, exactly as a dyn-with-body.

The token is NOT reserved in this commit. Reserved words are what a value span stops at, globally,
and TypeName.equals still carries `case` inside its host-opaque match; reserving now would break its
round-trip. 3b reserves it in the same commit that converts TypeName — the last site — so the corpus
is never in a state where the word is reserved and still opaque somewhere.

PROJECT

A maximal run of consecutive case elements in a body is ONE dispatch: `<scrutinee> match { … }`.
Branch heads take the four forms — `case a: T =>`, `case _: T =>`, `case e =>`, `case _ =>`. The
scrutinee is never authored: in an actor's message it is the message, which this projection names
`msg`; in a dyn-with-body it is the dyn's SINGLE parameter, and a dyn carrying cases with any other
number of parameters is refused rather than guessed at. That rule was found by building and is now
in drake.dlt.

Whether a run gets a catch-all follows from the statements-or-result split the spec already had: a
run of EFFECT branches (no branch carries a value) gets `case _ => ()` when no default was authored,
because a non-matching scrutinee is a no-op and the target owes it; a run of RESULT branches gets
none, because exhaustiveness there is the author's. A case in a rule action or a factory body is an
error naming the two places it is admitted, not a silent fall-through to the BodyElement catch-all —
which is exactly how a `loc` in a rule action was lost once before.

